    "Observations:\n",
    "1. English language dominates the data. It has 16.6% of the pages but 55.6% of all views (62 billion views), about 4,700 views per page per day. Spanish is next at about 1,280 and Media (Commons/MediaWiki) is the lowest at about 146.\n",
    "2. Every language has a weekly cycle. It is clearest in Spanish, where the daily line swings sharply every week and the level dips around the turn of the year and in mid-2016.\n",
    "3. English shows a campaign effect. From July to September 2016 the 7-day average jumps from about 4,500 to about 8,000, exactly where the campaign days (red shading) cluster. Isolated campaign days elsewhere show smaller bumps.\n",
    "4. Other languages have one-off spikes with no campaign flag. Russian (July-August 2016, roughly 3x normal), French (April 2016), and Chinese and Japanese (January-February 2016). We have no exogenous variable for these.\n",
    "5. Volatility: Russian is the most volatile (CV 0.35) because of its summer spike, followed by Media (0.25). German, Japanese and Chinese are the steadiest (CV about 0.12-0.13).\n",
    "6. Why English is so much larger: The maximum views for a single page on a single day exceed 17 million, while the median page gets about 100-160. A small number of very popular English pages therefore drive the average."
