    "## Results: comparison across languages\n",
    "\n",
    "Orders were chosen by grid search on AIC using training data only. Every model is scored on the same last 60 days, against a seasonal-naive baseline (repeat the last observed week).\n",
    "\n",
    "| Language | Best model | MAPE % | Seasonal-naive MAPE % | Gain vs naive |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Chinese | SARIMA | 5.27 | 4.50 | -17% |\n",
    "| English | SARIMAX | 6.06 | 9.22 | 34% |\n",
    "| French | Prophet | 6.48 | 11.14 | 42% |\n",
    "| German | SARIMA | 6.43 | 7.50 | 14% |\n",
    "| Japanese | SARIMA | 5.76 | 7.41 | 22% |\n",
    "| Russian | ARIMA | 6.93 | 10.05 | 31% |\n",
    "| Media (Commons/MediaWiki) | ARIMA | 12.22 | 16.47 | 26% |\n",
    "| Spanish | SARIMA | 13.48 | 16.77 | 20% |\n",
    "\n",
    "Observations:\n",
    "1. Six of eight languages fall inside the 4-8% MAPE range. Spanish (13.5%) and Media (12.2%) do not.\n",
    "2. Spanish is hard because the test window (November-December) contains the highest day of the whole series (9 November) and the December drop in level. The forecast misses that spike and keeps the autumn weekly swing while actual views fall. The data covers only 1.5 years, so a yearly pattern cannot be identified.\n",
    "3. Chinese: the seasonal-naive baseline (4.50%) beats every fitted model. The series is flat and stable, so a model adds nothing.\n",
    "4. Russian and Media were best fitted by ARIMA, which gives an almost flat forecast. Their errors come from unforecastable spikes rather than from the weekly cycle."
